<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiang Ziya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Jiang Taigong, Lü Shang, Lord Taigong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiang Ziya, also known as Jiang Taigong, was a minister and military adviser to Kings Wen and Wu of Zhou in the eleventh century BCE. Tradition credits him with leading the Zhou forces to victory over the Shang at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muye">Battle of Muye</a>, after which he was enfeoffed with the State of Qi in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. He became a legendary figure of Chinese strategy and religion, and the Six Secret Teachings were attributed to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiang-ziya.jpg" alt="Jiang Ziya" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiang_Ziya.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The first ruler of Qi bore the given name Shang, with the ancestral surname Jiang and the lineage surname Lü, and the courtesy names Shangfu, esteemed father, and Ziya. Sima Qian's Records of the Grand Historian calls him by his posthumous title, the Grand Lord of Qi. According to the traditional account, he served the Shang court for about twenty years before he found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">King Zhou of Shang</a> unbearable, feigned madness and withdrew. He is said to have reappeared in the Zhou countryside at the apocryphal age of seventy-two, fishing in a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> with a barbless hook or none at all, waiting for the fish to come to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a> found him fishing after a divination foretold that he would meet a teacher sent by Heaven. Their talk on war and statecraft is said to form the Six Secret Teachings. King Wen made him his chief minister and gave him the title Taigong Wang, the hope of the Grand Duke, after a prophecy of his grandfather. Jiang's daughter Yi Jiang married King Wu and bore him sons, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-cheng-of-zhou">King Cheng of Zhou</a>.</p><p>After King Wen's death Jiang counselled King Wu to wait for the right moment. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muye">Battle of Muye</a>, some 35 kilometres from the Shang capital Yin, he led the charge with 100 men, and many of the Shang troops surrendered or revolted. He stayed loyal to the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Ji Dan</a>, the Duke of Zhou, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-three-guards">Rebellion of the Three Guards</a>, and set his seat in Qi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingqiu">Yingqiu</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. Under him Qi is said to have thrived on its fish and salt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Jiang Ziya has been worshipped as a war god since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-tang">Emperor Xuanzong of Tang</a> he had a state temple as the patron of soldiers, and candidates for the military examinations and generals worshipped him. The Six Secret Teachings count among the Seven Military Classics of Ancient China. In the Ming novel Investiture of the Gods he is a Taoist adept who defeats the Shang's supernatural protectors and deifies 365 major gods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiang Ziya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji, King Wu of Zhou enfeoffed his leading general and adviser <b>Jiang Ziya</b>, known as Lord Taigong, with land in the north of the Shandong peninsula, where he established his capital at Yingqiu, traditionally identified with Linzi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Lu, King Cheng's brother the State of Jin, the Duke of Shao's family the State of Yan, and <b>Jiang Ziya</b> the State of Qi; the philosopher Xunzi later wrote that the Duke of Zhou set up 71 states, 53 of them held by members of the Ji clan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ju-county">Ju County</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.58,118.83&amp;year=-1035" aria-label="Ju County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jiang Ziya"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4919 4523h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4919 4523h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.58,118.83&amp;year=-1035">What was here in 1035 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
